  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 224 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will remain anchored across the Four Corners \nregion the through the first of next week. This will allow for some \nmonsoonal-subtropical moisture to rotate north and then eastward \nacross our area around the ridge periphery. This will bring near \ndaily chances for at least isolated to scattered thunderstorms. \nEnsemble guidance maintains the stronger winds aloft farther north \nacross South Dakota the first of the week, with those shifting \nsouthward some by the middle and latter part of next week. Mean PWAT \nvalues remain on the high side through much of the week, generally \nin the 1.2 to 1.4\" range. With the stronger winds aloft remaining to \nour north, severe storm activity seems low. However, locally heavy \nrainfall could be a concern due to the abundance of moisture and \nslow moving nature of the storms. \n\nTemperatures will be quite warm Sunday and Monday, with highs \nnearing the century mark, especially Sunday across southwest \nNebraska. As the ridge flattens some, and translates from the \nFour Corners to the souther plains by the middle and latter parts\nof the week, temperatures will likely cool as a cold front sags\nsouthward through the area.",
